WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.870
Joan's technique first.

2
00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:06.744
Read to the end: the real question is often in the last line.

3
00:00:06.744 --> 00:00:09.093
Rule out the obviously wrong options.

4
00:00:09.093 --> 00:00:11.315
Always and never are usually wrong.

5
00:00:11.315 --> 00:00:16.458
For the best response, pick the one that's most ethical, legal and compassionate,

6
00:00:16.458 --> 00:00:17.284
the gentlest.

7
00:00:17.284 --> 00:00:20.268
You're a student, so tell the R N can be right.

8
00:00:20.268 --> 00:00:22.871
And ignore runs of C: answers are random.

9
00:00:23.321 --> 00:00:24.938
Now the classes, both ways.

10
00:00:24.938 --> 00:00:28.889
She said most of them start with anti, meaning it stops something.

11
00:00:29.339 --> 00:00:30.299
<v Examiner>Anti-emetic?

12
00:00:30.749 --> 00:00:33.435
Prevents or stops nausea and vomiting.

13
00:00:33.435 --> 00:00:34.989
Emesis means vomiting.

14
00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:36.403
A very common group.

15
00:00:36.853 --> 00:00:37.834
<v Examiner>Antihistamine?

16
00:00:38.284 --> 00:00:40.119
Blocks histamine, for allergy.

17
00:00:40.569 --> 00:00:41.763
<v Examiner>Bronchodilator?

18
00:00:42.213 --> 00:00:44.411
Opens the airways, like salbutamol.

19
00:00:44.861 --> 00:00:45.757
<v Examiner>Diuretic?

20
00:00:46.207 --> 00:00:47.764
Increases urine output.

21
00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:48.950
<v Examiner>Analgesic?

22
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:49.833
<v Examiner>Antipyretic?

23
00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:51.158
<v Examiner>Anti-inflammatory?

24
00:00:51.608 --> 00:00:52.656
Relieves pain.

25
00:00:52.656 --> 00:00:53.705
Reduces fever.

26
00:00:53.705 --> 00:00:55.277
Reduces inflammation.

27
00:00:55.727 --> 00:00:57.007
<v Examiner>A contraindication?

28
00:00:57.457 --> 00:01:00.265
A reason not to give a drug to this person,

29
00:01:00.265 --> 00:01:03.400
like a known penicillin allergy for amoxicillin.

30
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.967
Definition plus example.

31
00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:08.019
Now her formative keys that haven't come up yet.

32
00:01:08.469 --> 00:01:10.773
<v Examiner>The benefit of an enteric-coated tablet?

33
00:01:11.223 --> 00:01:13.805
It resists the digestive action of the stomach.

34
00:01:14.255 --> 00:01:17.903
<v Examiner>Most drugs and their metabolites are excreted by which organ?

35
00:01:18.353 --> 00:01:18.927
The kidney.

36
00:01:18.927 --> 00:01:20.337
She called that one a gift.

37
00:01:20.787 --> 00:01:22.643
<v Examiner>How are topical medicines given?

38
00:01:23.093 --> 00:01:24.906
Directly on to a body surface.

39
00:01:25.356 --> 00:01:30.884
<v Examiner>Which is given by the oral route: codeine elixir, a corticosteroid ointment,

40
00:01:30.884 --> 00:01:34.593
<v Examiner>a paracetamol suppository, or an insulin injection?

41
00:01:35.043 --> 00:01:36.029
Codeine elixir.

42
00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:38.265
An elixir is a liquid you swallow.

43
00:01:38.715 --> 00:01:43.259
<v Examiner>Which approach best supports health literacy when teaching about a new medicine?

44
00:01:43.709 --> 00:01:46.909
Plain language, and confirm understanding with teach-back.

45
00:01:47.359 --> 00:01:51.647
<v Examiner>Which entity monitors the quality and safety of medicines in Aotearoa?

46
00:01:52.097 --> 00:01:52.886
Medsafe.

47
00:01:53.336 --> 00:01:56.680
<v Examiner>A drug that binds a receptor and causes a response?

48
00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.712
<v Examiner>One that binds and causes none?

49
00:01:59.162 --> 00:01:59.988
An agonist.

50
00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:01.039
An antagonist.

51
00:02:01.489 --> 00:02:03.132
<v Examiner>What does A D R stand for?

52
00:02:03.582 --> 00:02:05.075
Adverse drug reaction.

53
00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:10.816
<v Examiner>For a drug with very high first-pass metabolism, which route would not be prescribed?

54
00:02:11.266 --> 00:02:12.013
Oral.

55
00:02:12.463 --> 00:02:16.982
The classes both ways: anti-emetic stops nausea and vomiting;

56
00:02:16.982 --> 00:02:23.428
antihistamine blocks histamine; bronchodilator opens airways; diuretic increases urine;

57
00:02:23.428 --> 00:02:27.207
analgesic relieves pain; antipyretic reduces fever.

58
00:02:27.207 --> 00:02:29.874
Enteric coating resists the stomach.

59
00:02:29.874 --> 00:02:32.171
Most drugs leave by the kidney.

60
00:02:32.171 --> 00:02:34.468
Topical goes on a body surface.

61
00:02:34.468 --> 00:02:36.246
Medsafe monitors safety.

62
00:02:36.246 --> 00:02:38.839
Always and never are usually wrong.
